                    <blockquote>A Mola Mola usually swims around sea jellies and other native food it consumes.</blockquote>
                    <p><span class="firstcharacter">M</span>ola Mola is one of the heaviest bony fish in the world. One fine adult weighs around 1,000 kg making it one of the behemoth underwater, with its humongous size. A Mola Mola usually swims around sea jellies and other native food it consumes. No wonder, this creature is vulnerable to sea waste as most of them appear to be similar to jellyfish.Frequently, Molas are entangled in fishing nets, making them.</p>
                    <p>Mola Mola moves wherever the ocean current would take them. And in their life cycle, a female Mola Mola could lay up to 300,000 eggs. Baby Mola Molas grow in size at a fast pace. It took them to keep themselves warm in their native environment, Mola Mola sunbathe in shallow water most of the time. This is why they are often mistaken for sharks when their huge dorsal fins emerge above the water. While swimming at warmer temperatures, seagulls and other small reef fish  (e.g. cichlids or bannerfish)help Mola Mola by fending off parasites of their body.</p>
                    <p>Are they friendly? We will say that they are curious enough to approach divers. Fear not, they are harmless. Diving with Mola Mola has been one of the tropical underwater checklists. Knowing so, it’s not that hard to tick it off your diving checklist. One of the best spots to encounter Mola-Mola is Bali. There are many diving spots where you can make friends with Mola Molas! Get ready to start this amazing adventure!</p>
